===Noun===
{{wp|data,+ reference}}
{{wikidata lexeme|L6496}}
{{en-noun|data<q:sense 1 and 2>|s<q:senses 3 and 4>}}

# Something known or assumed as [[fact]], and is made the basis of [[reasoning]] or [[inference]] which an [[intellectual]] system of any sort (such as [[knowledge]] or [[theoretical]] [[framework]]) is constructed.
#: {{ux|en|a '''datum''' of experience}}
#: {{ux|en|given this '''datum''', it follows that}}
# {{senseid|en|singular of data}} {{singular of|en|data<id:plural of datum>|cap=1}}: A single recorded [[phenomenon]], especially obtained by [[scientific]] work.
#: {{syn|en|data point}}
#: {{hol|en|data set}}
# {{lb|en|cartography|surveying|engineering|manufacturing}} A [[point]], [[line]], [[plane]], or [[surface]] with [[reference]] to which [[position]]s (such as [[elevation]]s) are [[measure]]d or [[indicate]]d. {{ng|(Examples include a permanent [[benchmark]] in [[level]]ing or [[mean]] sea level in a [[topographical]] [[survey]])}}.
#: {{ux|en|A geodetic '''datum''' is sometimes also called a benchmark.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[GD&T]] tells us that the center of a {{w|bolt circle}} is in fact defined by the locations of the holes themselves, as each is a '''datum'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2000|publisher=International Boundaries Research Unit, University of Durham|author=Nuno Sergio Marques Antunes|journal=Maritime Briefing|volume=2|number=7|title=The Importance of the Tidal Datum in the Definition of Maritime Limits and Boundaries|page=5|text=In a strict sense, a ''tidal '''datum''''' can be understood as the reference plane (or surface) to which the height of a predicted tide is referred.{{...}}Sounding and chart '''datums''' are low water '''datums''', that is, they refer to the level of the water surface at low tide. Nonetheless, there are also '''datums''' based on high water levels.{{...}}These two different '''datums''' may be included in the broader category of ''vertical '''datums''''', which comprises any plane or surface used as a reference to measure vertical distances (such as depths, drying features, heights on shore, etc.).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Roger F Tomlinson|title=Thinking about GIS: geographic information system planning for managers|passage='''Datums''' are another important map aspect related to projection. A '''datum''' provides a base reference for measuring locations on Earth's surface.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A floating [[reference point]], or [[SLDMB]], used to [[evaluate]] surface [[current]]s in a [[body of water]], and often employed by coastal search and rescue.

